[ 0 ] = 0\n\nTherefore, the dot product is zero, which implies that the vectors A\u2081C\u2081 and B\u2081D\u2081 are perpendicular. Hence, the lines A\u2081C\u2081 and B\u2081D\u2081 are perpendicular.\n\nWow, that worked out! By expressing the dot product in terms of trigonometric identities and substituting the angles, everything cancels out, resulting in zero. Therefore, the vectors are indeed perpendicular, which means the lines A\u2081C\u2081 and B\u2081D\u2081 are perpendicular. This completes the proof.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{A_{1}C_{1} \\perp B_{1}D_{1}}", "deepseek_solution": "Given points \\( A, B, C, D \\) on a circle in the specified order, and \\( A_{1}, B_{1}, C_{1}, D_{1} \\) are the midpoints of the arcs \\( AB, BC, CD, \\) and \\( DA \\) respectively, we need to prove that \\( A_{1}C_{1} \\perp B_{1}D_{1} \\).\n\n1. **Parametrization of the Circle**:\n   - Consider the circle as the unit circle in the complex plane.\n   - Assign angles \\( \\theta_A, \\theta_B, \\theta_C, \\theta_D \\) to points \\( A, B, C, D \\) respectively.\n   - The midpoints of the arcs \\( AB, BC, CD, \\) and \\( DA \\) are given by:\n     - \\( A_1 \\) at angle \\( \\frac{\\theta_A + \\theta_B}{2} \\)\n     - \\( B_1 \\) at angle \\( \\frac{\\theta_B + \\theta_C}{2} \\)\n     - \\( C_1 \\) at angle \\( \\frac{\\theta_C + \\theta_D}{2} \\)\n     - \\( D_1 \\) at angle \\( \\frac{\\theta_D + \\theta_A}{2} + \\pi \\) (mod \\( 2\\pi \\))\n\n2. **Simplifying the Angles**:\n   - Assume the circle is parameterized such that \\( \\theta_A = 0 \\).\n   - Let the arcs \\( AB, BC, CD, \\) and \\( DA \\) be \\( 2a, 2b, 2c, \\) and \\( 2d \\) respectively, where \\( a + b + c + d = \\pi \\).\n   - The midpoints are then:\n     - \\( A_1 \\) at angle \\( a \\)\n     - \\( B_1 \\) at angle \\( 2a + b \\)\n     - \\( C_1 \\) at angle \\( 2a + 2b + c \\)\n     - \\( D_1 \\) at angle \\( \\pi + a + b + c \\)\n\n3. **Complex Coordinates**:\n   - Represent the midpoints as complex numbers:\n     - \\( A_1 = e^{ia} \\)\n     - \\( B_1 = e^{i(2a + b)} \\)\n     - \\( C_1 = e^{i(2a + 2b + c)} \\)\n     - \\( D_1 = -e^{i(a + b + c)} \\)\n\n4. **Vectors and Dot Product**:\n   - Calculate vectors \\( A_1C_1 \\) and \\( B_1D_1 \\):\n     - Vector \\( A_1C_1 \\): \\( (\\cos(2a + 2b + c) - \\cos a, \\sin(2a + 2b + c) - \\sin a) \\)\n     - Vector \\( B_1D_1 \\): \\( (-\\cos(a + b + c) - \\cos(2a + b), -\\sin(a + b + c) - \\sin(2a + b)) \\)\n   - Compute the dot product of these vectors and simplify using trigonometric identities:\n     - The dot product simplifies to zero, confirming perpendicularity.\n\nThus, the lines \\( A_1C_1 \\) and \\( B_1D_1 \\) are perpendicular, proving the statement.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A_{1}C_{1} \\perp B_{1}D_{1}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Let \\( O \\) be the center of the circle on which points \\( A, B, C, D \\) lie in that order.\n2. \\( A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1 \\) are the midpoints of the arcs \\( AB, BC, CD, DA \\) respectively.\n   Since the circle is divided into these arcs, we can define the angular measurements of these arcs as follows:\n   \\[ \\alpha = \\text{arc} \\, AB, \\quad \\beta = \\text{arc} \\, BC, \\quad \\gamma = \\text{arc} \\, CD, \\quad \\delta = \\text{arc} \\, DA. \\]\n3. We observe that the points \\( A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1 \\) are midpoints of their respective arcs.\n4. Note that:\n   \\[ A_1 \\text{ is the midpoint of arc } AB \\implies \\angle AOB = 2 \\alpha \\text{ and } \\angle A_1OB_1 = \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2}. \\]\n   Similarly:\n   \\[ B_1 \\text{ is the midpoint of arc } BC \\implies \\angle BOC = 2 \\beta \\text{ and } \\angle B_1OC_1 = \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2}. \\]\n   \\[ C_1 \\text{ is the midpoint of arc } CD \\implies \\angle COD = 2 \\gamma \\text{ and } \\angle C_1OD_1 = \\frac{\\gamma + \\delta}{2}. \\]\n   \\[ D_1 \\text{ is the midpoint of arc } DA \\implies \\angle DOA = 2 \\delta \\text{ and } \\angle D_1OA_1 = \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2}. \\]\n5. To prove that \\( A_1C_1 \\perp B_1D_1 \\), calculate the angle between \\( A_1C_1 \\) and \\( B_1D_1 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1OC_1 + \\angle B_1OD_1 = \\left( \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2} + \\frac{\\beta + \\gamma}{2} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{\\gamma + \\delta}{2} + \\frac{\\delta + \\alpha}{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n6. Simplify the addition:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1OC_1 + \\angle B_1OD_1 = \\frac{2\\alpha + 2\\beta + 2\\gamma + 2\\delta}{2} = \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta\n   \\]\n7. Considering that \\( A, B, C, D \\) make a complete circle:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n8. Thus, the angle between \\( A_1C_1 \\) and \\( B_1D_1 \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{360^\\circ}{2} = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n9. But we are looking for the perpendicularity, which implies two lines intersect at right angles, i.e., \\( 90^\\circ \\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle A_1OC_1 = 90^\\circ \\implies A_1C_1 \\text{ is perpendicular to } B_1D_1.\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{A_1C_1 \\perp B_1D_1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Can we solve every cubic equation using only basic operations and root extraction based on the previous discussion? (See problem 65 in section IV as well)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the question is whether every cubic equation can be solved using only basic operations and root extraction, based on the previous discussion and problem 65 in section IV. Let me try to work through this step by step.\n\nFirst, I remember that cubic equations are polynomials of degree three. The general form is ax\u00b3 + bx\u00b2 + cx + d = 0. The question is about solving these using basic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) and root extractions (like square roots, cube roots, etc.). \n\nFrom what I recall, quadratic equations can be solved using the quadratic formula, which involves square roots. So maybe there's a similar formula for cubics? I think there is something called Cardano's formula. Let me try to remember how that works.\n\nCardano's method involves reducing the general cubic equation to a depressed cubic (a cubic without the x\u00b2 term). This is done by a substitution. For example, if we have x\u00b3 + ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0, we can let x = y - a/3, which eliminates the y\u00b2 term. Then we get a depressed cubic in terms of y: y\u00b3 + py + q = 0. \n\nOnce in this form, Cardano's formula tells us that the solution can be written in terms of cube roots. Specifically, the solution involves taking the cube root of expressions that include square roots. So, in essence, we need both square roots and cube roots to express the solution. \n\nBut wait, the question is about using \"only basic operations and root extraction\". If by root extraction they mean any radicals (square roots, cube roots, etc.), then maybe the answer is yes. However, I need to confirm whether the process of solving the cubic requires only these operations or if there's something more involved.\n\nLet me break down the steps in Cardano's method:\n\n1. Start with the general cubic equation.\n2. Make a substitution to remove the x\u00b2 term (Tschirnhaus substitution).\n3. The depressed cubic is then solved using a formula that involves cube roots and square roots.\n\nSo, each step uses basic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) and root extractions (square and cube roots). Therefore, it seems possible. However, there might be some cases where the formula involves complex numbers even when the roots are real. For example, the casus irreducibilis, where three real roots exist, but to express them using radicals, you have to go through complex numbers. \n\nBut the question is about whether we can solve every cubic equation using these operations, not necessarily whether the intermediate steps might involve complex numbers. So even if we have to take roots of complex numbers, as long as the final result can be expressed in terms of real radicals (if possible), maybe it's still considered solvable by radicals. \n\nWait, but the original problem might be referring to real roots. In the case of casus irreducibilis, the real roots can't be expressed using only real radicals; they require complex numbers in the intermediate steps. However, in the context of algebra, when we talk about solvability by radicals, complex numbers are allowed in the radicals. So even though the intermediate steps use complex numbers, the formula still counts as a solution using radicals.\n\nTherefore, if we allow complex numbers in the root extractions, then yes, every cubic equation can be solved using radicals. But if we restrict ourselves to real radicals only, then there are cases where it's impossible. However, the question doesn't specify whether we need real roots or allow complex operations. Since the problem is in the context of previous discussions (problem 65 in section IV), I should consider what was discussed there.\n\nAssuming problem 65 might have discussed Cardano's method or similar, it's likely that the answer is yes, every cubic can be solved with basic operations and root extractions (including complex radicals). So the answer would be yes, based on the existence of Cardano's formula, which uses substitution, division, addition, and extraction of square and cube roots.\n\nBut let me verify this. For example, take the equation x\u00b3 - 3x + 1 = 0. This is a depressed cubic. Applying Cardano's formula, we calculate the discriminant. The discriminant for a depressed cubic y\u00b3 + py + q = 0 is -4p\u00b3 - 27q\u00b2. Here, p = -3, q = 1. So discriminant is -4*(-3)\u00b3 - 27*(1)\u00b2 = -4*(-27) -27 = 108 -27 =81. Since the discriminant is positive, there are three real roots. But according to casus irreducibilis, when there are three real roots and the discriminant is positive, the roots can't be expressed using real radicals alone. So, in this case, Cardano's formula would require taking cube roots of complex numbers. \n\nHowever, complex numbers can be expressed in terms of radicals if we include square roots of negative numbers. So technically, even though the roots are real, we need to use complex numbers in the process. But the final expressions for the real roots can be written using trigonometric functions instead of radicals, but that's not the same as radicals. \n\nBut the question is about using only basic operations and root extractions. If we allow complex roots (i.e., square roots of negative numbers), then even in the casus irreducibilis, we can express the solutions using radicals. For example, using the formula involving cube roots of complex numbers which themselves can be written using square roots. Therefore, in the algebraic sense, these equations are solvable by radicals, even if the radicals are complex.\n\nTherefore, the answer is yes, every cubic equation can be solved using basic operations and root extractions, as per Cardano's method, even if it requires complex radicals in the process. The casus irreducibilis doesn't prevent solvability by radicals; it just means the radicals are complex.", "deepseek_solution": "Yes, every cubic equation can be solved using only basic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) and root extractions (square roots, cube roots, etc.). This is achieved through **Cardano's method**, which systematically reduces the general cubic equation to a simpler form and expresses solutions using radicals. Here's the breakdown:\n\n1. **Depressed Cubic Transformation**:  \n   The general cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) is transformed into a \"depressed cubic\" \\( y^3 + py + q = 0 \\) via the substitution \\( x = y - \\frac{b}{3a} \\). This eliminates the \\( y^2 \\) term.\n\n2. **Cardano's Formula**:  \n   The depressed cubic is solved using the formula:  \n   \\[\n   y = \\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{q}{2} + \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{q}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^3}} + \\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{q}{2} - \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{q}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^3}}.\n   \\]  \n   This involves **square roots** (under the radical sign) and **cube roots**.\n\n3. **Casus Irreducibilis**:  \n   For cases with three real roots (when the discriminant \\( \\Delta = -4p^3 - 27q^2 > 0 \\)), the solution requires **complex numbers** in intermediate steps. However, complex numbers can still be expressed using radicals (e.g., \\( \\sqrt{-1} \\)), so the roots are ultimately expressible via basic operations and radicals.\n\n4. **Algebraic Solvability**:  \n   By definition, cubic equations are algebraically solvable by radicals. Complex intermediates are permitted in classical algebra, so the requirement for basic operations and root extractions is satisfied.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nAll cubic equations can indeed be solved using basic operations and root extractions, as demonstrated by Cardano's method. This includes cases requiring complex radicals (for three real roots), which are still valid within the framework of algebraic solutions.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's consider the general cubic equation:\n\n$$\na x^{3} + b x^{2} + c x + d = 0\n$$\n\nWe attempt to express the left-hand side of the equation in the form:\n\n$$\nr(x + s)^{3} - t(x + u)^{3}\n$$\n\nBy expanding and comparing coefficients, we derive the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr - t & = a, \\\\\nrs - tu & = \\frac{b}{3}, \\\\\nrs^2 - tu^2 & = \\frac{c}{3}, \\\\\nrs^3 - tu^3 & = d.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving this system, we find that $s$ and $u$ are the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\n$$\nX^{2} - AX + B = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA & = \\frac{bc - 9ad}{b^2 - 3ac}, \\\\\nB & = \\frac{c^3 - 3bd}{b^2 - 4ac}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing this, we have:\n\n$$\ns - u = \\sqrt{A^2 - 4B}\n\\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n\\\\text{s + u = A}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's write our cubic equation as:\n\n$$\nr(x+s)^3 = t(x+u)^3,\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root on both sides, we get:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{r}(x+s) = \\varrho \\sqrt[3]{t}(x+u),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\varrho$ is a cube root of unity. Solving for $x$, we have:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{\\varrho u \\sqrt[3]{t} - s \\sqrt[3]{r}}{\\sqrt[3]{r} - \\varrho \\sqrt[3]{t}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\varrho$ can take the values:\n\n$$\n\\varrho_1 = -\\frac{1}{2} - i \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\quad\n\\varrho_2 = -\\frac{1}{2} + i \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\quad\n\\varrho_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nBy squaring and noting the properties of cube roots of unity, we confirm:\n\n$$\n\\varrho_1^2 = \\varrho_2, \\quad\n\\varrho_2^2 = \\varrho_1, \\quad\n\\varrho_1 + \\varrho_2 + \\varrho_3 = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions to the cubic equation are:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{(\\varrho u \\sqrt[3]{t} - s \\sqrt[3]{r})(\\sqrt[3]{r^2} + \\varrho \\sqrt[3]{rt} + \\varrho^2 \\sqrt[3]{t^2})}{t-r},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently:\n\n$$\nx = -\\frac{b}{3a} + \\frac{\\varrho}{a} \\sqrt{-C^2 D} + \\frac{\\varrho^2}{a} \\sqrt{-C D^2},\n$$\n\nwhere;\n\n$$\nC = \\frac{b}{3} - au  \\quad \\text{and } \\quad  D = \\frac{b}{3} - as.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this form is complex; therefore, the equation is often simplified first. By dividing each term by $a$ and eliminating the quadratic term through substitution, we reach a simpler form: \n\n$$\nx^3 + px + q = 0\n$$\n\nUsing the above form, we directly substitute $a, b, c, d$ with $1, 0, p, q$, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{3q}{p}, \\\\\nB &= -\\frac{p}{3}, \\\\\nu, s &= \\frac{3q}{2p} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3q}{2p}\\right)^{3} + \\frac{p}{3}}, \\\\\nC &= -u, \\\\\nD &= -s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis yields:\n\n$$\nx = \\varrho \\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{q}{2} + \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{q}{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^2}} + \\varrho^2 \\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{q}{2} - \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{q}{2}\\right)^{2} + \\left(\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^2}}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is known as the Cardano Formula.\n\n### Conclusion:\nIndeed, every cubic equation can be solved using basic operations and roo