's verify: 3pi/2 is pi + pi/2, so cos(3pi/2 + alpha). Using cos(3pi/2 + alpha) = sin alpha. Wait, earlier I had:\n\ncos(3pi/2 + alpha) = sin alpha. Let me check this again.\n\nAlternatively, using the identity for cos(A + B):\n\ncos(3pi/2 + alpha) = cos3pi/2 cos alpha - sin3pi/2 sin alpha\n\ncos3pi/2 = 0, sin3pi/2 = -1\n\nTherefore, 0 * cos alpha - (-1) * sin alpha = sin alpha. Correct.\n\nTherefore, cos^3(3pi/2 + alpha) = sin^3 alpha.\n\nThus, the second term is -4 * cos alpha * sin^3 alpha.\n\nTherefore, the expression becomes:\n\n4 sin alpha cos^3 alpha - 4 cos alpha sin^3 alpha\n\nFactor out 4 sin alpha cos alpha:\n\n4 sin alpha cos alpha (cos^2 alpha - sin^2 alpha)\n\nThen, using identities:\n\nsin alpha cos alpha = (1/2) sin2alpha\n\ncos^2 alpha - sin^2 alpha = cos2alpha\n\nTherefore, 4 * (1/2) sin2alpha * cos2alpha = 2 sin2alpha cos2alpha = sin4alpha\n\nYes, that seems correct. Let me plug in a specific angle to test.\n\nLet me choose alpha = 0.\n\nOriginal expression:\n\n4 cos(-pi/2) sin^3(pi/2) - 4 sin(5pi/2) cos^3(3pi/2)\n\ncos(-pi/2) = cos(pi/2) = 0\n\nsin^3(pi/2) = 1^3 = 1\n\nSo first term: 4*0*1 = 0\n\nSecond term: sin(5pi/2 - 0) = sin(5pi/2) = 1\n\ncos(3pi/2 + 0) = cos(3pi/2) = 0\n\nSo second term: -4*1*0^3 = 0\n\nTotal expression: 0 - 0 = 0\n\nSimplified expression: sin4*0 = sin0 = 0. Correct.\n\nAnother test: alpha = pi/4\n\nOriginal expression:\n\nFirst term:\n\n4 cos(pi/4 - pi/2) sin^3(pi/2 + pi/4) = 4 cos(-pi/4) sin^3(3pi/4)\n\ncos(-pi/4) = sqrt(2)/2\n\nsin(3pi/4) = sqrt(2)/2, so cubed is (sqrt(2)/2)^3 = (2^(1/2))/8 = 2^(3/2)/8 = sqrt(2)/4\n\nSo first term: 4 * sqrt(2)/2 * (sqrt(2)/4) = 4 * (sqrt(2)/2) * (sqrt(2)/4) = 4 * (2/8) = 4 * (1/4) = 1\n\nSecond term:\n\n-4 sin(5pi/2 - pi/4) cos^3(3pi/2 + pi/4)\n\nsin(5pi/2 - pi/4) = sin(5pi/2 - pi/4) = sin(9pi/4) = sin(pi/4) = sqrt(2)/2\n\ncos(3pi/2 + pi/4) = cos(7pi/4) = sqrt(2)/2, cubed is (sqrt(2)/2)^3 = sqrt(2)/4\n\nSo second term: -4 * (sqrt(2)/2) * (sqrt(2)/4) = -4 * (2/8) = -4 * (1/4) = -1\n\nTotal