)^(-2/3)*(2x) = (2x)/(3 (x\u00b2 - 9)^(2/3)).\n\nTherefore, by L\u2019H\u00f4pital\u2019s Rule, the limit becomes:\n\n[1/(2 sqrt(x + 13)) - 1/sqrt(x + 1)] / [ (2x)/(3 (x\u00b2 - 9)^(2/3)) ] as x approaches 3.\n\nBut plugging x = 3 into this new expression: numerator becomes [1/(2 sqrt(16)) - 1/sqrt(4)] = [1/(8) - 1/2] = (1/8 - 4/8) = -3/8.\n\nDenominator becomes (2*3)/(3 (9 - 9)^(2/3)) ) = (6)/(3*0) = 6/0, which is undefined. Wait, so we get denominator approaching infinity? So the whole expression would be (-3/8) divided by something approaching infinity, so the limit would be 0. So this confirms the previous result. So the limit is 0.\n\nBut wait, let me check again. The denominator after applying L\u2019H\u00f4pital is (2x)/(3 (x\u00b2 - 9)^(2/3)). As x approaches 3, x\u00b2 - 9 approaches 0, so (x\u00b2 - 9)^(2/3) approaches 0, so denominator approaches (2*3)/(3*0) = 6/(0) which is infinity. Therefore, the entire expression becomes (-3/8)/infinity, which is 0. So yes, limit is 0.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another method? Let's rationalize the numerator. The numerator is sqrt(x + 13) - 2 sqrt(x + 1). Let me multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate. But the conjugate here is a bit tricky because there are two terms. Maybe multiply by sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1). Let's try:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1):\n\nNumerator becomes [sqrt(x + 13) - 2 sqrt(x + 1)][sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1)] = (sqrt(x + 13))\u00b2 - (2 sqrt(x + 1))\u00b2 = (x + 13) - 4(x + 1) = x + 13 - 4x - 4 = -3x + 9 = -3(x - 3).\n\nDenominator becomes [sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1)] * cube root(x\u00b2 - 9).\n\nSo now the expression becomes:\n\n[-3(x - 3)] / [ (sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1)) * cube root(x\u00b2 - 9) ]\n\nSo we have:\n\n-3(x - 3) / [ (sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1)) * (x\u00b2 - 9)^(1/3) ]\n\nNote that x\u00b2 - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3). So we can factor that:\n\nDenominator: (sqrt(...)) * ( (x - 3)(x + 3) )^(1/3) = (sqrt(...)) * (x - 3)^(1/3) * (x + 3)^(1/3)\n\nTherefore, the expression is:\n\n-3(x - 3) / [ (sqrt(...)) * (x - 3)^(1/3) * (x + 3)^(1/3) ) ] = -3 / [ (sqrt(...)) * (x + 3)^(1/3) ) ] * (x - 3) / (x - 3)^(1/3) )\n\nSimplify (x - 3)/(x - 3)^(1/3) = (x - 3)^(1 - 1/3) = (x - 3)^(2/3)\n\nTherefore, the expression becomes:\n\n-3 / [ (sqrt(x + 13) + 2 sqrt(x + 1)) * (x + 3)^(1/3) ) ] * (x - 3)^(2/3)\n\nNow, as x approaches 3, (x - 3)^(2/3) approaches 0. The rest of the terms: sqrt(x + 13) approaches sqrt(16) = 4, 2 sqrt(x + 1) approaches 2*sqrt(4) = 4, so sqrt(...) + 2 sqrt(...) approaches 4 + 4 = 8. (x + 3)^(1/3) approaches (6)^(1/3). Therefore, the coefficient becomes -3 / (8 * 6^(1/3)), and multiplied by (x - 3)^(2/3) which approaches 0. Therefore, the entire expression approaches 0. So again, limit is 0.\n\nSo all three methods: substitution with Taylor expansion, L\u2019H\u00f4pital\u2019s Rule, and algebraic manipulation lead to the conclusion that the limit is 0.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another approach with substitution. Let x = 3 + t, where t approaches 0. Then as before, denominator becomes cube root( (3 + t)^2 - 9 ) = cube root(9 + 6t + t\u00b2 - 9 ) = cube root(6t + t\u00b2) \u2248 cube root(6t) for small t. So denominator \u2248 (6t)^(1/3).\n\nNumerator: sqrt(3 + t + 13) - 2 sqrt(3 + t + 1) = sqrt(16 + t) - 2 sqrt(4 + t). As before, which we approximated to -3t/8. So numerator \u2248 -3t/8. Then numerator / denominator \u2248 (-3t/8)/( (6t)^(1/3) ) = (-3/(8*6^(1/3))) * t / t^(1/3) = (-3/(8*6^(1/3))) * t^(2/3) \u2192 0 as t \u2192 0.\n\nSame result.\n\nTherefore, all methods consistently show that the limit is 0. So I think the answer is 0.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me check with numerical values. Let's take x approaching 3 from both sides.\n\nFor example, take x = 3.1 (approaching from the right). Compute numerator and denominator.\n\nNumerator: sqrt(3.1 +13) - 2*sqrt(3.1 +1) = sqrt(16.1) - 2*sqrt(4.1). sqrt(16.1) \u2248 4.01248, sqrt(4.1) \u2248 2.02485. So numerator \u2248 4.01248 