ay**, the subsequent Fridays are the 9th, 16th, 23rd, and 30th.\n   - The **16th** is also a Friday, confirming three even Fridays (2nd, 16th, 30th).\n\n3. **Determine the 18th**:\n   - The 16th is Friday. Adding two days:  \n     - **17th** \u2192 Saturday  \n     - **18th** \u2192 Sunday  \n\n**Answer**: The 18th of the month is a **Sunday**.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's analyze the given problem: Three Fridays in a certain month fell on even dates. We need to determine the day of the week for the 18th of that month.\n\n2. Since three Fridays fall on even dates in the same month, it implies that these dates are distributed in such a way that they include three Fridays within one month under the constraint of even dates.\n\n3. In a typical month, there are a sequence of dates from 1 to 28 or from 1 to 30 or 31. We need to understand the distribution of Fridays on the even dates within this month.\n   \n4. If there are three Fridays on even dates, those dates must be distinct, and hence they must occur over a span such that at least three even-numbered dates are Fridays.\n\n5. Analyzing the given even-numbered dates in the month:\n   - Consider the sequence of Fridays that could potentially be on even dates: 2, 16, and 30.\n   - These dates have to be evenly spaced by weeks (7 days apart) considering that a Friday recurs every 7 days.\n\n6. For the first Friday to be on an even date, let's assume the first even Friday:\n   - If the first Friday was on the 2nd of the month, the next Fridays hence would be on 2 + 7 days = 9 ->  (which is not even), next 2 + 14 = 16 (even), next 2 + 21 = 23 -> (which is not even) and next 2 + 28 = 30 (even).\n\n7. Given the assumption that the three Fridays are on the 2nd, 16th, and 30th of the month:\n   - This satisfies the condition of having three Fridays on even dates: 2, 16, 30.\n   \n8. Now to find the day of the week for the 18th:\n   - Since 2 is Friday -> next one's (9th) Saturday -> then (16th) Friday -> then (23rd) Friday ->  then (30th) Friday.\n   \n9. Calculating Day of the week for the 18th:\n   - 2nd (Friday) + 7 days = 9 (Saturday) + 7 days = 16 (Friday)\n   - From (16th) Friday to the 18th is the 3rd day; so the 17th would be Saturday, then 18th would be a Sunday.\n   \n**Conclusion:**\n18th is a Sunday.\n\nThus, the day of the week for 18 in such a month is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Sunday}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If a positive function \\( f(x) \\) defined on a set \\( D \\) satisfies the following inequality for any \\( x, y \\in D \\):\n$$\nf(x) f(y) \\leqslant\\left[f\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)\\right]^{2}\n$$\n(with equality if and only if \\( x = y \\)), then for any \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\in D \\), the following inequality holds:\n$$\nf(x_1) f(x_2) \\cdots f(x_n) \\leqslant\\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n}\\right)\\right]^n\n$$\n(with equality if and only if \\( x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n \\)).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that if a positive function f(x) defined on a set D satisfies the inequality f(x)f(y) \u2264 [f((x+y)/2)]\u00b2 for any x, y in D, with equality if and only if x = y, then for any n variables x\u2081, x\u2082, ..., x\u2099 in D, the product f(x\u2081)f(x\u2082)...f(x\u2099) is less than or equal to [f((x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099)/n)]\u207f, with equality when all x_i are equal. \n\nFirst, I need to understand what the given condition implies about the function f. The inequality f(x)f(y) \u2264 [f((x+y)/2)]\u00b2 seems related to some sort of convexity or concavity property. Maybe log-concave or log-convex? Let me recall that a function is log-concave if log(f) is concave, which would mean that log(f(x)) + log(f(y)) \u2264 2 log(f((x+y)/2)). That's exactly the given inequality if we take logs of both sides. So taking logs, the given inequality becomes log(f(x)) + log(f(y)) \u2264 2 log(f((x+y)/2)), which is equiva