ply Jensen's inequality directly for the logarithm. Let's recall that for a concave function \u03c6, we have \u03c6( (x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099)/n ) \u2265 (\u03c6(x\u2081) + ... + \u03c6(x\u2099))/n. If \u03c6 = log(f), then log(f( (x\u2081 + ... + x\u2099)/n )) \u2265 (log(f(x\u2081)) + ... + log(f(x\u2099)))/n. Exponentiating both sides gives f( (x\u2081 + ... + x\u2099)/n ) \u2265 [f(x\u2081)...f(x\u2099)]^(1/n). Raising both sides to the nth power gives [f( (x\u2081 + ... + x\u2099)/n )]^n \u2265 f(x\u2081)...f(x\u2099), which is exactly the inequality we need. So this seems like a straightforward application of Jensen's inequality for the concave function log(f). \n\nBut wait, in the problem statement, they mention that the equality holds if and only if all x_i are equal. That also makes sense with Jensen's inequality because for a strictly concave function, equality in Jensen's holds if and only if all the variables are equal. Since the given condition says that equality holds if and only if x = y, which would imply that log(f) is strictly concave. Therefore, if log(f) is strictly concave, then Jensen's inequality gives us strict inequality unless all x_i are equal. \n\nSo putting it all together, since log(f) is (strictly) concave, applying Jensen's inequality directly gives the desired result. Therefore, the solution is straightforward once we recognize that the given condition implies log-concavity. But let me verify this step by step to make sure.\n\nFirst, given f(x)f(y) \u2264 [f( (x + y)/2 )]\u00b2 with equality iff x = y. Taking natural logarithm on both sides gives ln(f(x)) + ln(f(y)) \u2264 2 ln(f( (x + y)/2 )). This is the definition of a concave function for ln(f). Specifically, a function \u03c6 is concave if for any x, y and \u03bb \u2208 [0,1], \u03c6(\u03bbx + (1 - \u03bb)y) \u2265 \u03bb\u03c6(x) + (1 - \u03bb)\u03c6(y). Here, we have the midpoint case (\u03bb = 1/2), so \u03c6( (x + y)/2 ) \u2265 (\u03c6(x) + \u03c6(y))/2. If this ho