ft(x_{m+2}\\right) f\\left(x_{m+3}\\right) \\cdots f\\left(x_{2m+1}\\right) f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{k+1}}{k+1}\\right)\\right] \\\\\n      & \\leqslant \\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{m+1}}{m+1}\\right)\\right]^{m+1} \\cdot \\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_{m+2} + \\cdots + x_{2m+1}}{m+1} + \\frac{\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{2m+1}}{2m+1}}{m+1}\\right)\\right]^{m+1} \\\\\n      & \\leqslant \\left\\{\\left[f\\left(\\frac{\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{2m+1}}{2(m+1)} + \\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{2m+1}}{2m+1}}{2}\\right)\\right]^{2}\\right\\}^{m+1} \\\\\n      & = \\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{2m+1}}{2m+1}\\right)\\right]^{2(m+1)} \\\\\n      & = \\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_{k+1}}{k+1}\\right)\\right]^{k+2}.\n      \\end{aligned}\n      \\]\n\n3. Conclusion: Since we have shown that the inequality holds for \\(n = 1, 2, \\ldots, k\\) (by induction), it now holds for \\(n = k + 1\\). By the principle of mathematical induction, the inequality holds for all \\(n \\in \\mathbf{N}\\).\n\nThus, we conclude that:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{f\\left(x_1\\right) f\\left(x_2\\right) \\cdots f\\left(x_n\\right) \\leqslant \\left[f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n}\\right)\\right]^n} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Is it possible to place the digits 0, 1, and 2 in the cells of a $100 \\times 100$ grid of squared paper in such a way that each $3 \\times 4$ rectangle, whose sides align with the cell edges, contains exactly three zeros, four ones, and five twos?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if it's possible to arrange the digits 0, 1, and 2 in a 100x100 grid such that every 3x4 rectangle contains exactly three zeros, four ones, and five twos. Let me start by breaking down the problem and considering the constraints.\n\nFirst, the grid is 100x100, which is a large grid, but the key here is the 3x4 subgrids. Each of these subg