h radius 7 meters, but when it reaches the adjacent vertices (B and C), the rope would start to wrap around the triangle.\n\nBut since the rope is longer than the side length (7 > 5), after going around vertex B, the remaining rope is 7 - 5 = 2 meters. Similarly, after going around vertex C, remaining rope is 2 meters. So, the grazing area would be a combination of sectors.\n\nLet me think. When the sheep is at vertex A, it can swing around the outside of the triangle. The total angle around vertex A outside the triangle would be 360 degrees minus the 60 degrees of the triangle's internal angle. But wait, the triangle is in the way, so the sheep can't graze the 60 degrees sector that's inside the triangle. So, the initial sector is 300 degrees (360 - 60) with radius 7 meters.\n\nBut then, when the sheep moves along the sides AB or AC, beyond the vertices B or C, the rope can extend beyond those vertices. Since the rope is 7 meters and each side is 5 meters, once the sheep goes around vertex B, it has 7 - 5 = 2 meters of rope left to graze around B. Similarly for vertex C.\n\nHowever, when the sheep goes around vertex B, the angle it can cover around B is 60 degrees, because the triangle's internal angle is 60 degrees. Wait, no. When the sheep goes around vertex B, how much angle can it cover? Let's think.\n\nAfter going around vertex B, the sheep can swing around vertex B with the remaining 2 meters of rope. The external angle at vertex B is 120 degrees (since the internal angle is 60 degrees, the external angle is 180 - 60 = 120 degrees). Wait, but actually, when the sheep is moving from vertex A around vertex B, the path it can take is constrained by the triangle. Once it reaches vertex B, it can swing around B with the remaining rope length. The angle it can cover around B would be 240 degrees? Wait, no. Let's clarify.\n\nWhen the sheep is tied to A with a 7-meter rope, it can move around the outside of the triangle. When it reaches vertex B, the rope is fully ex