n k. Let me write it as:\n\n-3 k\u00b2 + (8 c - 2 a)k + (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - 4 c\u00b2) = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n3 k\u00b2 + (-8 c + 2 a)k + (-a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + 4 c\u00b2) = 0\n\nSo:\n\n3 k\u00b2 + (2 a - 8 c)k + (4 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2) = 0\n\nThis quadratic can be solved for k. Let me compute discriminant D:\n\nD = (2a - 8c)^2 - 4 * 3 * (4 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2)\n\nCompute term by term:\n\n(2a - 8c)^2 = 4a\u00b2 - 32 a c + 64 c\u00b2\n\n4 * 3 * (4 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2) = 12 (4 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2) = 48 c\u00b2 - 12 a\u00b2 - 12 b\u00b2\n\nSo, D = (4a\u00b2 - 32 a c + 64 c\u00b2) - (48 c\u00b2 - 12 a\u00b2 - 12 b\u00b2) = 4a\u00b2 -32 a c +64 c\u00b2 -48 c\u00b2 +12 a\u00b2 +12 b\u00b2\n\nSimplify:\n\n(4a\u00b2 +12a\u00b2) + (-32 a c) + (64 c\u00b2 -48 c\u00b2) +12 b\u00b2\n\n=16a\u00b2 -32 a c +16 c\u00b2 +12 b\u00b2\n\nFactor:\n\n16(a\u00b2 - 2 a c + c\u00b2) +12 b\u00b2 =16(a - c)^2 +12 b\u00b2\n\nSo, discriminant D =16(a - c)^2 +12 b\u00b2. Since this is always positive, there are two real solutions for k. But since E is on BC between B(0,0) and C(c,0), k must be between 0 and c. So, we need to check which of the roots falls into that interval.\n\nThe solutions for k are:\n\nk = [ - (2a - 8c) \u00b1 \u221aD ] / (2 * 3) = [8c - 2a \u00b1 \u221a(16(a - c)^2 +12 b\u00b2)] /6\n\nSimplify \u221aD:\n\n\u221a(16(a - c)^2 +12 b\u00b2) = \u221a[4*(4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2)] = 2 \u221a[4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2]\n\nSo,\n\nk = [8c - 2a \u00b1 2\u221a(4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2)] /6 = [4c - a \u00b1 \u221a(4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2)] /3\n\nSo, two possible solutions for k. Let me denote them as k1 and k2:\n\nk1 = [4c - a + \u221a(4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2)] /3\n\nk2 = [4c - a - \u221a(4(a - c)^2 +3 b\u00b2)] /3\n\nWe need to check if these k's lie between 0 and c.\n\nBut this might get complicated. Maybe there's a better approach than coordinate geometry. Let's think about using mass point geometry or trigonometry.\n\nGiven that angle C is twice angle