roper divisors of any natural number.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There are many identical squares. In the vertices of each square, the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4 are written in a random order. The squares are stacked, and the sum of the numbers that fell into each of the four corners of the stack is written down. Is it possible that:\n\na) In each corner of the stack, the sum is equal to 2004?\n\nb) In each corner of the stack, the sum is equal to 2005?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so there's this problem about squares with numbers 1, 2, 3, 4 on their vertices, arranged randomly. Then we stack many such squares, and in each corner of the stack, we add up the numbers from each square that landed there. The question is whether it's possible for all four corner sums to be 2004 or 2005. Hmm, interesting.\n\nLet me try to visualize this. Each square has four corners, each labeled with 1, 2, 3, 4 in some order. When you stack them, each position in the stack (the four corners) gets the sum of the numbers from each square's corresponding corner. So if we stack 'n' squares, each corner's sum is the sum of n numbers, each from 1 to 4, depending on how each square was rotated or arranged.\n\nWait, but the squares are identical except for the rotation, right? Because they're just squares with numbers 1-4 on vertices. So each square can be rotated in different ways\u20140\u00b0, 90\u00b0, 180\u00b0, 270\u00b0\u2014which permutes the numbers on the corners. So the key is that each square is the same, but rotated arbitrarily, and when stacked, each corner's sum is 2004 or 2005.\n\nSo the question is, can we choose rotations for each square in the stack such that all four corner sums are equal to 2004 or 2005? The problem has parts (a) and (b), so maybe one is possible and the other isn't. Let me think.\n\nFirst, maybe there's a parity issue here. Let's analyze the total sum. Each square contributes 1+2