n   - The angle with the x-axis is \\( \\arctan(5.675) \\approx 80^\\circ \\).\n\nThus, the acute angle between \\( MN \\) and \\( PA \\) is \\( \\boxed{E} \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe start by illustrating the problem context in $\\triangle PAT$ with $\\angle P=36^\\circ$, $\\angle A=56^\\circ$, and \\(PA=10\\).\n\n1. Compute the remaining angle $\\angle T$ in $\\triangle PAT$:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle T = 180^\\circ - \\angle P - \\angle A = 180^\\circ - 36^\\circ - 56^\\circ = 88^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n2. Points \\(U\\) and \\(G\\) lie on sides \\(TP\\) and \\(TA\\) respectively, with \\(PU = AG = 1\\). Points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are midpoints of \\(PA\\) and \\(UG\\).\n\n3. Extend \\(PN\\) to point \\(Q\\) such that \\(PN = NQ\\). Connect points \\(AQ\\) and \\(GQ\\).\n\n    \\[\n    \\text{From } PN = NQ \\text{ and } UN = NG,\n    \\]\n\n   - By the properties of midpoints and congruence of triangles:\n   \n     \\[\n     \\triangle PNU \\cong \\triangle QNG \n     \\]\n\n   This implies:\n\n     \\[\n     \\angle UPN = \\angle GQN \\quad \\text{and} \\quad QG = PU = AG.\n     \\]\n\n   Hence, \\(PU \\parallel GQ\\).\n\n4. Given the angles:\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle T = \\angle QGT = 2 \\angle QAT.\n    \\]\n\n   From the triangle properties and parallelism, we have:\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle TAQ = \\frac{\\angle T}{2} = \\frac{88^\\circ}{2} = 44^\\circ.\n    \\]\n\n5. As \\(PM = MA\\) and \\(PN = NQ\\), we get that \\(MN \\parallel AQ\\). We thus conclude:\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PMN = \\angle PAQ.\n    \\]\n\n6. Combining the angles, we get the angle formed by \\(MN\\) and \\(PA\\):\n\n    \\[\n    \\angle PAQ = \\angle PAT + \\angle TAQ = 56^\\circ + 44^\\circ = 100^\\circ.\n    \\]\n\n7. Finally, the angle complementary to \\(100^\\circ\\), which forms the vertical angle with line \\(PA\\), is:\n\n    \\[\n    180^\\circ - 100^\\circ = 80^\\circ.\n    \\]\n\nThus, the exterior angle between \\(MN\\) and \\(PA\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{80^\\circ}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the given curve at the point with abscissa $x_{0}$.\n\n$y=\\frac{x^{2}-3 x+6}{x^{2}}, x_{0}=3$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the equation of the normal line to the curve y = (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)/x\u00b2 at the point where x\u2080 = 3. Let me start by recalling what a normal line is. The normal line to a curve at a given point is the line that is perpendicular to the tangent line at that point. So, to find the normal line, I first need to find the slope of the tangent line at that point, then take the negative reciprocal of that slope to get the slope of the normal line. Once I have the slope of the normal line and the point (x\u2080, y\u2080), I can write the equation using the point-slope form.\n\nAlright, let me break this down step by step. First, I need to find the derivative of y with respect to x to get the slope of the tangent line. The function given is y = (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)/x\u00b2. Hmm, this looks like a rational function, so maybe I can simplify it first before differentiating. Let me try that.\n\nDividing each term in the numerator by x\u00b2, the function becomes:\n\ny = (x\u00b2/x\u00b2) - (3x)/x\u00b2 + 6/x\u00b2 = 1 - 3/x + 6/x\u00b2\n\nThat simplifies to y = 1 - 3x\u207b\u00b9 + 6x\u207b\u00b2. This might be easier to differentiate term by term.\n\nNow, let's find the derivative dy/dx. The derivative of 1 is 0. The derivative of -3x\u207b\u00b9 is 3x\u207b\u00b2. The derivative of 6x\u207b\u00b2 is -12x\u207b\u00b3. So putting that all together:\n\ndy/dx = 0 + 3x\u207b\u00b2 - 12x\u207b\u00b3 = 3/x\u00b2 - 12/x\u00b3\n\nAlternatively, I can write this with a common denominator:\n\ndy/dx = (3x - 12)/x\u00b3\n\nWait, let me check that. If I have 3/x\u00b2 - 12/x\u00b3, to combine these terms, the common denominator would be x\u00b3. So multiply the first term by x/x to get 3x/x\u00b3 - 12/x\u00b3 = (3x - 12)/x\u00b3. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should have used the quotient rule instead of simplifying first. Let me verify if both methods give the same result. The original function is y = (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)/x\u00b2. Using the quotient rule, which states that if y = u/v, then dy/dx = (u'v - uv')/v\u00b2.\n\nHere, u = x\u00b2 - 3x + 6, so u' = 2x - 3. And v = x\u00b2, so v' = 2x.\n\nApplying the quotient rule:\n\ndy/dx = [(2x - 3)(x\u00b2) - (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)(2x)] / (x\u00b2)\u00b2\n\nLet's compute the numerator step by step. First term: (2x - 3)(x\u00b2) = 2x\u00b3 - 3x\u00b2\n\nSecond term: (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)(2x) = 2x\u00b3 - 6x\u00b2 + 12x\n\nSo subtracting the second term from the first term:\n\n(2x\u00b3 - 3x\u00b2) - (2x\u00b3 - 6x\u00b2 + 12x) = 2x\u00b3 - 3x\u00b2 - 2x\u00b3 + 6x\u00b2 - 12x = (2x\u00b3 - 2x\u00b3) + (-3x\u00b2 + 6x\u00b2) + (-12x) = 0 + 3x\u00b2 - 12x\n\nTherefore, the numerator is 3x\u00b2 - 12x, and the denominator is x\u2074. So dy/dx = (3x\u00b2 - 12x)/x\u2074\n\nSimplifying this, factor numerator: 3x(x - 4). Denominator: x\u2074. So dy/dx = 3x(x - 4)/x\u2074 = 3(x - 4)/x\u00b3\n\nWait, but earlier when I simplified the function first, I got dy/dx = (3x - 12)/x\u00b3, which is the same as 3(x - 4)/x\u00b3. So both methods give the same result. Good, that confirms the derivative is correct.\n\nSo dy/dx = 3(x - 4)/x\u00b3. Now, we need to evaluate this derivative at x\u2080 = 3 to get the slope of the tangent line at that point.\n\nLet's compute dy/dx at x = 3:\n\ndy/dx = 3(3 - 4)/3\u00b3 = 3(-1)/27 = -3/27 = -1/9\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line at x = 3 is -1/9. Therefore, the slope of the normal line, which is the negative reciprocal, would be 9.\n\nWait, reciprocal of -1/9 is -9, and negative of that is 9. Yes, that's correct. So the slope of the normal line is 9.\n\nNow, we need a point through which the normal line passes. That point is (x\u2080, y\u2080), where x\u2080 = 3. We need to find y\u2080 by plugging x = 3 into the original equation.\n\nOriginal equation: y = (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)/x\u00b2\n\nPlugging in x = 3:\n\ny = (9 - 9 + 6)/9 = (6)/9 = 2/3\n\nSo the point is (3, 2/3).\n\nNow, with the slope of the normal line being 9 and passing through (3, 2/3), we can write the equation of the normal line using point-slope form:\n\ny - y\u2080 = m(x - x\u2080)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\ny - 2/3 = 9(x - 3)\n\nTo convert this into slope-intercept form (if needed), we can expand:\n\ny = 9x - 27 + 2/3\n\nConvert 27 to thirds: 27 = 81/3\n\nSo y = 9x - 81/3 + 2/3 = 9x - 79/3\n\nBut the question just asks for the equation of the normal line, so either form is acceptable unless specified otherwise. Since the problem doesn't specify, both forms are correct, but perhaps it's better to present it in a standard form. Alternatively, we can write it as:\n\ny = 9x - 79/3\n\nOr multiplying both sides by 3 to eliminate the fraction:\n\n3y = 27x - 79\n\nThen rearranged to standard form:\n\n27x - 3y - 79 = 0\n\nBut again, unless a specific form is required, point-slope or slope-intercept is fine. Let me check if the problem mentions the form. The original problem says \"Find the equation of the normal line...\", so likely any correct linear equation is acceptable, but perhaps they want it in a simplified form. Let me check my calculations again.\n\nWait, let me verify the point (3, 2/3). Original function: y = (x\u00b2 - 3x + 6)/x\u00b2. At x = 3, numerator is 9 - 9 + 6 = 6. Denominator is 9. 6/9 = 2/3. Correct.\n\nSlope of tangent: dy/dx at x=3 is -1/9. Therefore, normal slope is 9. Correct.\n\nEquation using point-slope: y - 2/3 = 9(x - 3). If I convert to standard form, I can write 9x - y - (27 - 2/3) = 0. Wait, 9x - y = 27 - 2/3. 27 is 81/3, so 81/3 - 2/3 = 79/3. Therefore, 9x - y = 79/3. Multiply both sides by 3: 27x - 3y = 79. So standard form is 27x - 3y - 79 = 0. Alternatively, slope-intercept form is y = 9x - 79/3.\n\nBut maybe the problem expects the answer in a particular format. Let me check if they want it in slope-intercept or another form. The problem says \"the equation of the normal line\", so both forms are correct. However, in calculus problems, often the answer is left in point-slope or simplified slope-intercept. Let me check the arithmetic again when expanding the point-slope form.\n\nStarting with y - 2/3 = 9(x - 3). Distribute the 9: y - 2/3 = 9x - 27. Add 2/3 to both sides: y = 9x - 27 + 2/3. Convert 27 to thirds: 27 = 81/3. So 81/3 - 2/3 = 79/3. Thus, y = 9x - 79/3. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, if they want it in fractions with denominator 3, but that's already the case. Alternatively, we can write it as y = 9x - (79/3). Either way, that's the equation.\n\nWait, let me confirm that the normal line is indeed perpendicular. The product of the slopes of the tangent and normal should be -1. The tangent slope is -1/9, normal slope is 9. Multiplying them: (-1/9)(9) = -1. Correct. So that checks out.\n\nJust to recap all steps:\n\n1. Simplify the function if possible to make differentiation easier.\n2. Find the derivative using either quotient rule or term-by-term differentiation after simplifying.\n3. Evaluate the derivative at x\u2080 = 3 to get the tangent slope.\n4. Take the negative reciprocal to get the normal slope.\n5. Find the y-coordinate at x\u2080 = 3 by plugging into the original function.\n6. Use point-slope form with the normal slope and the point to write the equation.\n7. Simplify if necessary.\n\nAll steps seem to check out. So the equation of the normal line is y = 9x - 79/3.\n\nAlternatively, if written as fractions:\n\ny = 9x - \\frac{79}{3}\n\nBut maybe they prefer it written as y = 9x - 26\u2153, but in mathematical contexts, improper fractions are usually preferred. So I think leaving it as y = 9x - 79/3 is appropriate.\n\nI can't see any mistakes in the calculations, so this should be the correct equation.", "deepseek_solution": "To find the equation of the normal line to the curve \\( y = \\frac{x^2 - 3x + 6}{x^2} \\) at \\( x_0 = 3 \\):\n\n1. **Simplify the function**:\n   \\[\n   y = 1 - \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{6}{x^2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Differentiate** to find the slope of the tangent line:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^{-2} - 12x^{-3} = \\frac{3x - 12}{x^3} = \\frac{3(x - 4)}{x^3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluate the derivative at \\( x_0 = 3 \\)**:\n   \\[\n   \\left. \\frac{dy}{dx} \\right|_{x=3} = \\frac{3(3 - 4)}{3^3} = \\frac{-3}{27} = -\\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n   The slope of the tangent line is \\( -\\frac{1}{9} \\).\n\n4. **Determine the slope of the normal line** (negative reciprocal):\n   \\[\n   m_{\\text{normal}} = 9\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the point on the curve** at \\( x_0 = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   y(3) = \\frac{3^2 - 3(3) + 6}{3^2} = \\frac{6}{9} = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n   The point is \\( (3, \\frac{2}{3}) \\).\n\n6. **Use point-slope form** to write the normal line equation:\n   \\[\n   y - \\frac{2}{3} = 9(x - 3)\n   \\]\n   Simplify to slope-intercept form:\n   \\[\n   y = 9x - \\frac{79}{3}\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 9x - \\frac{79}{3}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Find the derivative $y'$:**\n\n   Given the function:\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{x^2 - 3x + 6}{x^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the function:\n   \\[\n   y = 1 - \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{6}{x^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Differentiate term-by-term:\n   \\[\n   y' = \\left(1 - \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{6}{x^2}\\right)' = 0 + \\frac{3}{x^2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{6}{x^3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combine the derivatives:\n   \\[\n   y' = \\frac{3}{x^2} - \\frac{12}{x^3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Evaluate $y'$ at $x_0 = 3$:**\n\n   \\[\n   y'(x_0) = \\frac{3}{3^2} - \\frac{12}{3^3} = \\frac{3}{9} - \\frac{12}{27} = \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{4}{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the fractions:\n   \\[\n   y'(3) = \\frac{3}{9} - \\frac{4}{9} = -\\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluate $y$ at $x_0 = 3$:**\n\n   \\[\n   y(x_0) = \\frac{3^2 - 3 \\cdot 3 + 6}{3^2} = \\frac{9 - 9 + 6}{9} = \\frac{6}{9} = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Form the normal line equation:**\n\n   Since $y'(3) \\neq 0$, the equation of the normal line at $x_0 = 3$ is:\n   \\[\n   y - y_0 = -\\frac{1}{y'(x_0)} \\cdot (x - x_0)\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute:\n   \\[\n   y_0 = \\frac{2}{3}, \\quad y'(x_0) = -\\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   y - \\frac{2}{3} = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{9}} \\cdot (x - 3)\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   y - \\frac{2}{3} = 9(x - 3)\n   \\]\n\n   Distribute and isolate $y$:\n   \\[\n   y = 9(x - 3) + \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 9x - 27 + \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine the constant terms:**\n   \\[\n   y = 9x - 27 + \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 9x - \\frac{81}{3} + \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 9x - \\frac{79}{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the equation of the normal line is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 9x - \\frac{79}{3}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There is a bag containing 24 kg of nails. How can you measure out 9 kg of nails using only a balance scale without any weights?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to figure out how to measure out 9 kg of nails using a balance scale when all I have is a bag of 24 kg. Hmm, balance scale without any weights... So, the classic problem where you can divide things into two parts and compare them. I remember these kinds of problems often involve dividing the total into halves or thirds and then recombining them somehow. Let me start breaking it down.\n\nFirst, the total is 24 kg. The target is 9 kg. So, 9 is a fraction of 24. Let's see, 24 divided by 3 is 8, and 24 divided by 8 is 3. But 9 is 3/8 of 24. Not sure if that helps directly. Maybe I can find a way to split the nails into different groups and then use the balance to compare them.\n\nSince a balance scale allows me to check if two groups are equal, maybe I can create equal subsets and then use those to build up the 9 kg. Let's think step by step.\n\nFirst, divide the 24 kg into two equal parts. If I can split 24 into 12 and 12, that's a start. So, using the balance, I can put half on each side and make sure they're equal. Now I have two 12 kg piles. But 12 is still more than 9. How can I get to 9 from here?\n\nMaybe take one of the 12 kg piles and split that again. Split 12 into 6 and 6. Now I have two 6 kg piles. Hmm, 6 and 6. If I have 6 kg, maybe combine that with another portion. Wait, but 6 + 6 = 12, which I already have. How to get 9?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a different way to split the original 24 kg. Instead of splitting into 12 and 12 first, maybe split into different portions. Let me see. For example, if I can split 24 into three equal parts, each would be 8 kg. But how do I split into three parts with a balance scale? That's tricky because the balance can compare two sides. To split into three, maybe use multiple steps.\n\nWait, here's an idea. If I divide 24 into two parts of 8 and 16. Then, take the 16 and split into 8 and 8. Now I have three 8 kg piles. But how would I get 9 from that? 8 is close, but not 9. Alternatively, maybe split 24 into 9 and 15, but how? Without knowing the weight, that's the problem. I can't just take out 9 kg because I don't have a reference.\n\nWait, but I can create reference weights by dividing. Let's go back. Start with 24. Split into 12 and 12. Then, take one 12 and split into 6 and 6. Now we have 12, 6, 6. Then, take one 6 and split into 3 and 3. Now we have 12, 6, 3, 3. Then, take the 3 and combine with the 6? 3+6=9. But wait, how do I ensure that the 3 and 6 are accurate?\n\nWait, let's map this out step by step.\n\n1. Split 24 into 12 and 12. Keep one 12 aside.\n2. Take the other 12 and split into 6 and 6.\n3. Take one 6 and split into 3 and 3.\n4. Now, you have 12, 6, 3, 3.\n\nNow, if you take the 3 and the 6, that's 9. But how do you know that the 3 and 6 are accurate? Because each time you split, you use the balance to ensure the two sides are equal. So, when splitting 24 into 12 and 12, the balance confirms they're equal. Then splitting 12 into 6 and 6, balance confirms. Then 6 into 3 and 3. So each of those is accurate. Therefore, combining 3 and 6 gives 9 kg.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me verify. Start with 24. Split into two 12s. Take one 12, split into two 6s. Take one 6, split into two 3s. Now you have 12, 6, 3, 3. Then, take the 3 and the 6 and combine them: 3+6=9. So the remaining nails would be 24 - 9 = 15, which is the 12 and the other 3. So yes, this works.\n\nBut wait, is there another way? Let me check again. Another approach: Let's say instead of splitting 24 into 12 and 12 first, maybe split into different amounts. But without a reference, the only way to create known quantities is by repeatedly halving. So splitting into halves each time.\n\nAlternatively, think of 9 as 24 - 15. If I can measure 15, then subtract that from 24. But how to get 15? 15 is 12 + 3. Which again goes back to the previous method. So splitting 24 into 12 and 12, then 12 into 6 and 6, then 6 into 3 and 3. Then 12 + 3 = 15. Then 24 -15=9. So either way, whether you take 3+6 or 24 - (12+3), you get 9.\n\nSo the key is creating the 3 kg and 6 kg portions through successive halving, then combining them. Let me make sure there are no errors here. Each split is done by balancing, so each time you split a pile into two equal parts. Therefore, each split creates two equal halves, which are known quantities. So yes:\n\n- 24 splits to 12+12\n- 12 splits to 6+6\n- 6 splits to 3+3\n\nTherefore, you have 12, 6, 3, 3. Then 6 + 3 = 9. So that's the method.\n\nIs there any other way? Let's think again. Suppose you try to split 24 into three parts. Since a balance scale can compare two groups, maybe divide the 24 into three equal parts by splitting into two parts first and then using one part to measure the third. Wait, how?\n\nFor example, split 24 into 8 and 16. But how? Without a reference weight, how would you get 8? Unless you can use the parts you've already split. Let's see:\n\nIf you have 24, split into 12 and 12. Then, take 12 and split into 6 and 6. Then take 6 and split into 3 and 3. Now, if you have 3, 3, 6, 12. If you put 3 and 6 on one side (totaling 9) and then put the other side with 9 from the remaining nails. Wait, but how do you know the remaining nails are 9? Because the total is 24, so if you have 3+6=9 on one side, the other side should also be 9, but the remaining nails are 24 -9=15. Hmm, maybe that approach doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the balance to compare combinations. For example, take some nails from the 24 and balance them against a known combined weight. For instance, if you have 12 and 12, then 6 and 6, and 3 and 3. Suppose you take one 12, one 6, and one 3. That totals 21. Then the remaining would be 3 kg. But that's not helpful.\n\nWait, maybe another approach. If you have 12 and 12, then take one 12 and split into 9 and 3. But how do you get 9 from 12 without a reference? You need to create a known 3 kg to subtract. Which is what the previous method does. By splitting 6 into 3 and 3, you can then take 3 from the 12 (which was split into 6 and 6). Wait, let's see:\n\nOriginal splits:\n\n1. 24 \u2192 12 + 12\n2. Take one 12 \u2192 6 + 6\n3. Take one 6 \u2192 3 + 3\n\nNow, you have 12, 6, 3, 3.\n\nIf you take the other 12 (from step 1) and remove 3 kg from it, you get 9. But how do you remove 3 kg? Because you have a 3 kg reference. So, take the 12 and split it into two parts such that one part is 3 kg and the other is 9 kg. To do this, use the balance to compare parts of the 12 against the known 3 kg.\n\nSo, take the 12 kg pile and split it into two parts: one part on one side, and the 3 kg + some nails on the other side until they balance. Wait, actually, since you have a 3 kg reference, you can put the 3 kg on one side and then add nails to the other side until it balances. Wait, no. The balance scale compares two quantities. If you have a 3 kg weight, you can measure out 3 kg from another pile. But in this case, we don't have weights, only the nails. But we have created a 3 kg pile of nails. So, yes, we can use that 3 kg as a reference.\n\nTherefore, take the remaining 12 kg pile. To measure 9 kg from it, we can subtract 3 kg. So, put the 3 kg pile on one side, and then take nails from the 12 kg pile until it balances. That would give 3 kg from the 12, leaving 9 kg. So, that's another way. So total steps:\n\n1. Split 24 into 12 and 12.\n2. Split one 12 into 6 and 6.\n3. Split one 6 into 3 and 3.\n4. Now, use the 3 kg to measure 3 kg from the other 12 kg pile, leaving 9 kg.\n\nEither way, you end up with 9 kg. So this confirms that the method works. Either by combining 6 and 3, or by subtracting 3 from 12.\n\nSo the answer is to divide the nails into halves successively to get 12, 6, 3 kg portions, then combine 6 and 3 to make 9 kg. Or use the 3 kg to subtract from 12 kg to get 9 kg. Both methods are valid.\n\nLet me just check for any possible mistakes. Suppose I split 24 into 12 and 12. Then split one 12 into 6 and 6. Then split a 6 into 3 and 3. Now, if I take one 12 kg pile and remove 3 kg using the 3 kg reference, the remaining would be 9 kg. That works. Alternatively, take one 6 kg and one 3 kg pile, combine them to get 9 kg. Either approach is correct.\n\nYes, this seems solid. So the solution is to perform successive halving to create smaller known weights, then combine or subtract them as needed to reach the desired 9 kg.", "deepseek_solution": "To measure 9 kg of nails from a 24 kg bag using only a balance scale, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Split the 24 kg into two equal halves**:  \n   - Divide the nails into two 12 kg piles using the balance scale to ensure equality.  \n   - **Result**: 12 kg + 12 kg.\n\n2. **Split one 12 kg pile into two 6 kg piles**:  \n   - Take one of the 12 kg piles and split it into two equal 6 kg piles.  \n   - **Result**: 12 kg (remaining) + 6 kg + 6 kg.\n\n3. **Split one 6 kg pile into two 3 kg piles**:  \n   - Take one 6 kg pile and divide it into two equal 3 kg piles.  \n   - **Result**: 12 kg + 6 kg + 3 kg + 3 kg.\n\n4. **Combine the 6 kg and 3 kg piles**:  \n   - Merge the 6 kg and one 3 kg pile to total 9 kg.  \n   - **Final Result**: 9 kg (6 kg + 3 kg) and 15 kg (remaining nails).\n\n**Verification**:  \nEach split uses the balance scale to guarantee equal halves, ensuring accuracy. By systematically halving the original quantity, you derive precise smaller weights (12 kg, 6 kg, 3 kg). Combining 6 kg and 3 kg directly yields the required 9 kg. This method avoids guesswork and leverages the balance scale\u2019s ability to verify equality at each step.\n\n**Answer**:  \nMeasure 9 kg by splitting the nails into halves three times to obtain 6 kg and 3 kg, then combining them.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven that we have a bag with 24 kg of nails and a pair of balance scales without weights, we need to measure out exactly 9 kg of nails. The only available operation is to divide a quantity of nails into two equal weights. Below is a step-by-step method to achieve the goal.\n\n1. **Initial Condition:**\n    - We start with one pile of 24 kg.\n    \n    \\[\n    \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    & \\text{1st Pile} & \\text{2nd Pile} & \\text{3rd Pile} & \\text{4th Pile} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\text{Initially:} & 24 \\text{ kg} &  &  &  \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\end{array}\n    \\]\n\n2. **Step 1: Divide 24 kg into two 12 kg piles:**\n    - We divide the 24 kg pile into two equal piles of 12 kg each.\n    \n    \\[\n    24 \\text{ kg} \\div 2 = 12 \\text{ kg}\n    \\]\n    \n    \\[\n    \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    & \\text{1st Pile} & \\text{2nd Pile} & \\text{3rd Pile} & \\text{4th Pile} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\text{Step 1:} & 12 \\text{ kg} & 12 \\text{ kg} &  &  \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\end{array}\n    \\]\n\n3. **Step 2: Divide one of the 12 kg piles into two 6 kg piles:**\n    - Take one 12 kg pile and divide it further into two 6 kg piles.\n    \n    \\[\n    12 \\text{ kg} \\div 2 = 6 \\text{ kg}\n    \\]\n    \n    \\[\n    \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    & \\text{1st Pile} & \\text{2nd Pile} & \\text{3rd Pile} & \\text{4th Pile} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\text{Step 2:} & 12 \\text{ kg} & 6 \\text{ kg} & 6 \\text{ kg} &  \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\end{array}\n    \\]\n\n4. **Step 3: Divide one of the 6 kg piles into two 3 kg piles:**\n    - Take one 6 kg pile and divide it into two 3 kg piles.\n    \n    \\[\n    6 \\text{ kg} \\div 2 = 3 \\text{ kg}\n    \\]\n    \n    \\[\n    \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    & \\text{1st Pile} & \\text{2nd Pile} & \\text{3rd Pile} & \\text{4th Pile} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\text{Step 3:} & 12 \\text{ kg} & 6 \\text{ kg} & 3 \\text{ kg} & 3 \\text{ kg} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    \\end{array}\n    \\]\n\n5. **Step 4: Combine a 6 kg pile with a 3 kg pile to get 9 kg:**\n    - Finally, combine one of the 6 kg piles with one of the 3 kg piles to get the desired 9 kg pile.\n    \n    \\[\n    6 \\text{ kg} + 3 \\text{ kg} = 9 \\text{ kg}\n    \\]\n    \n    Thus, we have measured out exactly 9 kg of nails using the available operations.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9\\text{ kg}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "How many five-digit numbers of the form $\\overline{ab16c}$ are divisible by 16? ($a$, $b$, $c$ are arbitrary digits, not necessarily distinct).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to find how many five-digit numbers of the form ab16c are divisible by 16. Hmm. The number is structured as follows: the first digit is a, second is b, then 1, 6, and finally c. So, the number looks like a five-digit number where the thousands place is 1 and the hundreds place is 6. The other digits (a, b, c) can be any digits from 0 to 9, with the exception that a can't be 0 because it's a five-digit number. Wait, actually, the problem states that a, 