leanest way is to present the solutions as:\n\nt = 90\u00b0n,\n\nt =15\u00b0 +90\u00b0k,\n\nt =75\u00b0 +90\u00b0k,\n\nwhere n and k are integers.\n\nThis clearly indicates the three distinct sets of solutions.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is all t equal to 90\u00b0n, 15\u00b0 +90\u00b0k, or 75\u00b0 +90\u00b0k for integers n and k.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe solutions are \\(\\boxed{t = 90^{\\circ} n}\\), \\(\\boxed{t = 15^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ} k}\\), and \\(\\boxed{t = 75^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ} k}\\) for all integers \\(n\\) and \\(k\\).", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the trigonometric equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^{2}\\left(t + 45^{\\circ}\\right) - \\sin^{2}\\left(t - 30^{\\circ}\\right) - \\sin 15^{\\circ} \\cos\\left(2t + 15^{\\circ}\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t,\n\\]\n\nwe start by simplifying the left-hand side (LHS) using trigonometric identities. \n\nFirst, we use the identity for the difference of squares, \\(\\sin^2 A - \\sin^2 B = \\sin(A + B)\\sin(A - B)\\), with \\(A = t + 45^\\circ\\) and \\(B = t - 30^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2(t + 45^\\circ) - \\sin^2(t - 30^\\circ) = \\sin((t + 45^\\circ) + (t - 30^\\circ)) \\sin((t + 45^\\circ) - (t - 30^\\circ)) = \\sin(2t + 15^\\circ) \\sin(75^\\circ).\n\\]\n\nNext, we use \\(\\sin 75^\\circ = \\cos 15^\\circ\\) and rewrite the LHS:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(2t + 15^\\circ) \\cos 15^\\circ - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos(2t + 15^\\circ).\n\\]\n\nThis can be combined using the sine difference identity, \\(\\sin(A - B) = \\sin A \\cos B - \\cos A \\sin B\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin((2t + 15^\\circ) - 15^\\circ) = \\sin(2t).\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2t = 0.5 \\sin 6t.\n\\]\n\nWe use the triple-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 6t = 3 \\sin 2t - 4 \\sin^3 2t\\), leading to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2t = 0.5 (3 \\sin 2t - 4 \\sin^3 2t).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2t = 3 \\sin 2t - 4 \\sin^3 2t \\implies -\\sin 2t + 4 \\sin^3 2t = 0 \\implies \\sin 2t (4 \\sin^2 2t - 1) = 0.\n\\]\n\nThis gives two cases:\n1. \\(\\sin 2t = 0\\)\n2. \\(4 \\sin^2 2t - 1 = 0 \\implies \\sin 2t = \\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n\nFor \\(\\sin 2t = 0\\):\n- \\(2t = 180^\\circ n \\implies t = 90^\\circ n\\).\n\nFor \\(\\sin 2t = \\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\):\n- \\(2t = 30^\\circ + 360^\\circ k\\) or \\(150^\\circ + 360^\\circ k\\) (for \\(\\sin 2t = \\frac{1}{2}\\))\n- \\(2t = 210^\\circ + 360^\\circ k\\) or \\(330^\\circ + 360^\\circ k\\) (for \\(\\sin 2t = -\\frac{1}{2}\\))\n\nSolving for \\(t\\):\n- \\(t = 15^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) or \\(75^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) (from \\(\\sin 2t = \\frac{1}{2}\\))\n- \\(t = 105^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) or \\(165^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) (from \\(\\sin 2t = -\\frac{1}{2}\\))\n\nCombining all solutions and considering periodicity, we find the general solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = 90^\\circ n, \\quad t = 15^\\circ + 90^\\circ k, \\quad t = 75^\\circ + 90^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integers } n, k.\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe solutions are \\(\\boxed{t = 90^{\\circ} n}\\), \\(\\boxed{t = 15^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ} k}\\), and \\(\\boxed{t = 75^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ} k}\\) for all integers \\(n\\) and \\(k\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2\\left(t + 45^\\circ\\right) - \\sin^2\\left(t - 30^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos \\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n\\]\n\n1. **Transform each trigonometric term:**\n\n   Using the identity \\(\\sin^2 x = 1 - \\cos 2x\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2\\left(t + 45^\\circ\\right) = 1 - \\cos\\left(2t + 90^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2\\left(t - 30^\\circ\\right) = 1 - \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute these transformations into the equation:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(1 - \\cos\\left(2t + 90^\\circ\\right)\\right) - \\left(1 - \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right)\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos \\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   1 - \\cos\\left(2t + 90^\\circ\\right) - 1 + \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos\\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n   Combine like terms:\n\n   \\[\n   -\\cos\\left(2t + 90^\\circ\\right) + \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos\\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify using trigonometric identities:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos\\left(2t + 90^\\circ\\right) = -\\sin 2t\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the equation becomes:\n\n   \\[\n   -(-\\sin 2t) + \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos\\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t + \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos\\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use sum-to-product identities and angle sum formulas:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\cos\\left(2t - 60^\\circ\\right) = \\cos 2t \\cos 60^\\circ + \\sin 2t \\sin 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   - \\sin 15^\\circ \\cos\\left(2t + 15^\\circ\\right) = - \\sin 15^\\circ \\left(\\cos 2t \\cos 15^\\circ - \\sin 2t \\sin 15^\\circ\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute back into the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t + \\left(\\cos 2t \\cos 60^\\circ + \\sin 2t \\sin 60^\\circ\\right) - \\sin 15^\\circ \\left(\\cos 2t \\cos 15^\\circ - \\sin 2t \\sin 15^\\circ\\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute the exact values of trig functions:\n\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t + \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 2t + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin 2t \\right) - \\left( \\cos 2t \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} - \\sin 2t \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = 0.5 \\sin 6t\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplify to obtain the required terms, noting that:\n\n   \\[\n   2\\sin 2t - \\sin 6t = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Use the identity \\(\\sin 6t = 3 \\sin 2t - 4 \\sin^3 2t\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin 2t - (3 \\sin 2t - 4 \\sin^3 2t) = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin 2t - 3 \\sin 2t + 4 \\sin^3 2t = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\sin 2t + 4 \\sin^3 2t = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t (4 \\sin^2 2t - 1) = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve the equation \\(\\sin 2t = 0\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t = 0 \\Rightarrow 2t = 180^\\circ k \\Rightarrow t_1 = 90^\\circ k, \\quad k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve the equation \\(4 \\sin^2 2t - 1 = 0\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   4 \\sin^2 2t = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sin 2t = \\pm \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2t = \\pm 30^\\circ + 180^\\circ l \\Rightarrow t_2 = \\pm 15^\\circ + 90^\\circ l, \\quad l \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe solutions are:\n\\[\nt_1 = 90^\\circ k, \\quad t_2 = \\pm 15^\\circ + 90^\\circ l, \\quad \\text{where} \\quad k, l \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t_1 = 90^\\circ k, \\quad t_2 = \\pm 15^\\circ + 90^\\circ l, \\quad k, l \\in \\mathbb{Z}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Three points $A$, $B$, and $P$ are placed on a circle $\\Gamma$. Let $Q$ be the orthogonal projection of $P$ onto $(AB)$, $S$ the projection of $P$ onto the tangent to the circle at $A$, and $R$ the projection of $P$ onto the tangent to the circle at $B$. Show that $P Q^{2} = P R \\times P S$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. Let me read it again to make sure I understand. There's a circle \u0393 with three points A, B, and P on it. Then Q is the orthogonal projection of P onto the line AB. Then S is the projection of P onto the tangent at A, and R is the projection onto the tangent at B. The goal is to show that PQ squared equals PR times PS. Hmm, interesting. \n\nFirst, let me try to visualize the problem. There's a circle, three points on it. The projections are onto different lines: AB, the tangent at A, and the tangent at B. Since projections are involved, maybe some right triangles or similar triangles could come into play. Also, since all points are on a circle, maybe some properties of cyclic quadrilaterals or angles in a circle might be useful. \n\nLet me start by recalling that the tangent to a circle at a point is perpendicular to the radius at that point. So, the tangent at A is perpendicular to OA, where O is the center of the circle. Similarly, the tangent at B is perpendicular to OB. Since S and R are projections of P onto these tangents, maybe the coordinates or some coordinate geometry could help. Alternatively, maybe using vectors or trigonometry?\n\nAlternatively, maybe power of a point could be useful here. The power of point P with respect to the circle is zero because P is on the circle. But I don't see immediately how that connects. Wait, projections onto lines... Maybe some relations between the distances?\n\nLet me consider setting up a coordinate system. Let's suppose the circle \u0393 is the unit circle in the plane for simplicity, centered at the origin O(0,0). Let me assign coordinates to points A, B, and P. Let's say A is at (1,0), B is at some point (cos \u03b2, sin \u03b2), and P is at (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8). Then, Q is the projection of P onto AB, S is the projection onto the tangent at A, and R is the projection onto the tangent at B.\n\nFirst, let's find the equations of the tangent lines at A and B. The tangent at A(1,0) is perpendicular to OA, which is along the x-axis. So, the tangent at A is vertical, right? Because the radius OA is horizontal, so the tangent must be vertical. Hence, the tangent at A is the line x = 1. Similarly, the tangent at B(cos \u03b2, sin \u03b2) is perpendicular to OB. The slope of OB is (sin \u03b2)/(cos \u03b2) = tan \u03b2, so the slope of the tangent at B is -cot \u03b2. Therefore, the equation of the tangent at B can be written as y - sin \u03b2 = -cot \u03b2 (x - cos \u03b2). Let me verify that. The tangent line at B is perpendicular to OB, so its slope is -1/(tan \u03b2) = -cot \u03b2. Yes, that seems right.\n\nNow, S is the projection of P onto the tangent at A. Since the tangent at A is x = 1, the projection of P(cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) onto this line is the point S(1, sin \u03b8). Because the projection onto a vertical line x = 1 just changes the x-coordinate to 1 and keeps the y-coordinate the same. Similarly, R is the projection of P onto the tangent at B. To find R, we need to project P(cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) onto the tangent line at B: y - sin \u03b2 = -cot \u03b2 (x - cos \u03b2).\n\nLet me compute that projection. The formula for the projection of a point (x0, y0) onto the line ax + by + c = 0 is:\n\nThe projected point (x, y) can be calculated using:\n\nx = x0 - a*(a x0 + b y0 + c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\ny = y0 - b*(a x0 + b y0 + c)/(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)\n\nAlternatively, using vectors. Let me rewrite the tangent at B in standard form. Starting from y - sin \u03b2 = -cot \u03b2 (x - cos \u03b2). Multiply both sides by sin \u03b2 to eliminate the cotangent:\n\nsin \u03b2 (y - sin \u03b2) = -cos \u03b2 (x - cos \u03b2)\n\n=> sin \u03b2 y - sin\u00b2 \u03b2 = -cos \u03b2 x + cos\u00b2 \u03b2\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\ncos \u03b2 x + sin \u03b2 y - (cos\u00b2 \u03b2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b2) = 0\n\nBut cos\u00b2 \u03b2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b2 = 1, so the equation is:\n\ncos \u03b2 x + sin \u03b2 y - 1 = 0\n\nTherefore, the tangent at B is cos \u03b2 x + sin \u03b2 y = 1.\n\nSo, the coefficients are a = cos \u03b2, b = sin \u03b2, c = -1.\n\nTherefore, the projection R of P(cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8) onto this line is:\n\nx = cos \u03b8 - cos \u03b2*(cos \u03b2 cos \u03b8 + sin \u03b2 sin \u03b8 - 1)/(cos\u00b2 \u03b2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b2)\n\nSimilarly,\n\ny = sin \u03b8 - sin \u03b2*(cos \u03b2 cos \u03b8 + sin \u03b2 sin \u03b8 - 1)/(cos\u00b2 \u03b2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b2)\n\nSince cos\u00b2 \u03b2 + sin\u00b2 \u03b2 = 1, the denominators are 1. Therefore:\n\nx = cos \u03b8 - cos \u03b2*(cos \u03b2 cos \u03b8 + sin \u03b2 sin \u03b8 - 1)\n\ny = sin \u03b8 - sin \u03b2*(cos \u03b2 cos \u03b8 + sin \u03b2 sin \u03b8 - 1)\n\nSimplify the expressions inside:\n\ncos \u03b2 cos \u03b8 + sin \u03b2 sin \u03b8 = cos(\u03b2 - \u03b8) because cos(A - B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. So, that's cos(\u