rt(5)]/2)([1 - sqrt(5)]/2) = (1 -5)/4 = -1. So the new equation would be x\u00b2 - (sum)x + product = x\u00b2 -1x -1 = x\u00b2 -x -1. According to the given form, it should be x\u00b2 - p\u00b2x + pq = x\u00b2 -1x + (1)(-1) = x\u00b2 -x -1. That matches as well. So p=1 and q=-1 is also a solution.\n\nWait, but in this case, when q = -1, p can be either -2 or 1. However, when we set q = -1, we had to use both solutions from the quadratic equation for p. So both p =1 and p=-2 with q=-1 satisfy the conditions. However, when p=1, q can be any value, but in reality, when p=1, the condition requires q=-1? Wait, no. Wait, when p=1, from equation 2, we have (p -1)(q +1)=0, so if p=1, then q can be anything. But if p=1, let's check if the new equation actually requires q to be arbitrary. Wait, if p=1, then the original equation is x\u00b2 +x + q =0. The new equation is x\u00b2 -1x + q =0. But the new equation is supposed to have roots shifted by 1. Let's check with an example. Let\u2019s take p=1 and q=0. Then original equation is x\u00b2 +x =0, roots at 0 and -1. The new roots should be 1 and 0. So new equation is x\u00b2 - (1+0)x + (1*0)= x\u00b2 -x +0. Which matches x\u00b2 -1x +0. So according to the given form, when p=1, pq = 1*0=0, so the new equation is x\u00b2 -1x +0. Which is correct. So in this case, q=0 is allowed. But the problem states \"Calculate the coefficients of the equation\". So maybe there are multiple solutions? But the problem says \"the equation whose roots are one greater... is of the form x\u00b2 -p\u00b2x +pq=0\". So if p and q can take multiple values, but the problem doesn't specify any additional constraints, then there might be two solutions: one with p=1 and any q, and another with p=-2 and q=-1. Wait, but that doesn't make sense because when p=1 and q is arbitrary, shifting roots by 1 would require the new equation to have coefficients depending on q. But in the problem statement, the new equation is given as x\u00b2 -p\u00b2x + pq=0. So if p