( x \\) such that the sum of the digits of the sum \\( a \\) of the sequence \\( x, x+1, \\ldots, x+99 \\) is 50.\n\n1. **Calculate the sum \\( a \\)**:\n   The sum of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{\\text{number of terms}}{2} \\times (\\text{first term} + \\text{last term})\n   \\]\n   For the sequence \\( x, x+1, \\ldots, x+99 \\):\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{100}{2} \\times (x + (x + 99)) = 50 \\times (2x + 99) = 100x + 4950\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the digit sum condition**:\n   The sum of the digits of \\( a \\) must be 50. Since \\( a = 100x + 4950 \\), \\( a \\) ends in 50 (as \\( 100x \\) ends in 00 and adding 4950 results in 50). Therefore, the last two digits of \\( a \\) are 5 and 0, contributing 5 to the digit sum. The remaining digits must sum to 45.\n\n3. **Find the smallest \\( a \\)**:\n   For \\( a \\) to be a 7-digit number, the first five digits must sum to 45. The only way to achieve this is if all five digits are 9. Thus, the smallest 7-digit number ending in 50 with a digit sum of 50 is 9999950.\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\)**:\n   Set \\( a = 9999950 \\):\n   \\[\n   100x + 4950 = 9999950\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   100x = 9999950 - 4950 = 9995000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{9995000}{100} = 99950\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification**:\n   Check if smaller values of \\( x \\) result in a digit sum of 50. Decreasing \\( x \\) by 1 decreases \\( a \\) by 100, reducing the digit sum by 1 each time until it falls below 50. Thus, no smaller \\( x \\) satisfies the digit sum condition.\n\nThe smallest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{99950}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem \n\nGiven the sum \\( a \\) of one hundred natural numbers \\( x, x+1, x+2, \\cdots, x+99 \\), we know that the digit sum of \\( a \\) is 50. The problem asks for the smallest value of \\( x \\).\n\n\n1. **Sum of the Sequence:**\n\n    The sequence of numbers from \\( x \\) to \\( x+99 \\) is an arithmetic sequence. The sum o