t equation: \\( z = 1 - x - y \\). Substituting this into the manganese equation:\n\n\\[\n0.9y + 0.6(1 - x - y) = 0.4\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[\n9y + 6(1 - x - y) = 40 \\implies 9y + 6 - 6x - 6y = 40 \\implies 3y - 6x = -2 \\implies y = 2x - \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe also express \\( z \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nz = 1 - x - y = 1 - x - (2x - \\frac{2}{3}) = \\frac{5}{3} - 3x\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine the constraints on \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\):\n\n- \\( x \\geq \\frac{1}{3} \\) (since \\( y \\geq 0 \\))\n- \\( x \\leq \\frac{5}{9} \\) (since \\( z \\geq 0 \\))\n\nThe copper content in the new alloy is given by:\n\n\\[\n0.7x + 0.1y + 0.25z\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y \\) and \\( z \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n0.7x + 0.1(2x - \\frac{2}{3}) + 0.25(\\frac{5}{3} - 3x)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this expression:\n\n\\[\n0.7x + 0.2x - \\frac{1}{15} + \\frac{5}{12} - 0.75x = \\frac{3}{20}x + \\frac{7}{20}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Copper percentage} = \\frac{3x + 7}{20}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating this at the bounds of \\( x \\):\n\n- When \\( x = \\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3(\\frac{1}{3}) + 7}{20} = \\frac{1 + 7}{20} = \\frac{8}{20} = 40\\%\n\\]\n\n- When \\( x = \\frac{5}{9} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3(\\frac{5}{9}) + 7}{20} = \\frac{\\frac{15}{9} + 7}{20} = \\frac{\\frac{78}{9}}{20} = \\frac{26}{60} = \\frac{13}{30} \\approx 43.33\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the percentage of copper in the new alloy can range from 40% to 43\\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)%.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{40\\%} \\text{ to } \\boxed{43\\frac{1}{3}\\%}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let's denote the masses of the initial alloys as \\( m_{1} \\) (first alloy), \\( m_{2} \\) (second alloy), and \\( m_{3} \\) (third alloy), and the mass of the new alloy as \\( m \\). \n   \n   We have the following mass constraints:\n   \\[ m_{1} + m_{2} + m_{3} = m \\]\n\n2. From the problem, the percentage of manganese in the new alloy is 40%. This gives us the equation:\n   \\[ 0.9m_{2} + 0.6m_{3} =