ateral CDGE: C (2c, 2d), D (b + c, d), G (2(b + c)/3, 2d/3), E (c, d).\n\nLet me list the coordinates:\n\nC: (2c, 2d)\n\nD: (b + c, d)\n\nG: (2(b + c)/3, 2d/3)\n\nE: (c, d)\n\nTo find the area of quadrilateral CDGE, we can use the shoelace formula.\n\nFirst, list the coordinates in order. Let's go from C to D to G to E and back to C.\n\nSo the coordinates are:\n\nC (2c, 2d)\n\nD (b + c, d)\n\nG ( (2(b + c))/3, (2d)/3 )\n\nE (c, d)\n\nBack to C (2c, 2d)\n\nApplying the shoelace formula:\n\nArea = 1/2 |sum over i (x_i y_{i+1} - x_{i+1} y_i)|\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst segment: C to D:\n\nx_i = 2c, y_i = 2d\n\nx_{i+1} = b + c, y_{i+1} = d\n\nTerm: 2c * d - (b + c) * 2d = 2c d - 2d(b + c) = 2c d - 2b d - 2c d = -2b d\n\nSecond segment: D to G:\n\nx_i = b + c, y_i = d\n\nx_{i+1} = 2(b + c)/3, y_{i+1} = 2d/3\n\nTerm: (b + c) * (2d/3) - [2(b + c)/3] * d = (2d(b + c)/3) - (2d(b + c)/3) = 0\n\nThird segment: G to E:\n\nx_i = 2(b + c)/3, y_i = 2d/3\n\nx_{i+1} = c, y_{i+1} = d\n\nTerm: [2(b + c)/3] * d - c * (2d/3) = [2d(b + c)/3] - [2c d /3] = 2d(b + c - c)/3 = 2d b /3\n\nFourth segment: E to C:\n\nx_i = c, y_i = d\n\nx_{i+1} = 2c, y_{i+1} = 2d\n\nTerm: c * 2d - 2c * d = 2c d - 2c d = 0\n\nSum all terms: (-2b d) + 0 + (2b d /3) + 0 = (-2b d) + (2b d /3) = (-6b d /3 + 2b d /3) = (-4b d /3)\n\nTake absolute value and multiply by 1/2:\n\nArea = 1/2 * | -4b d /3 | = 1/2 * (4b d /3) = (2b d)/3\n\nNow, compute the area of triangle ABC. Using coordinates:\n\nA (0,0), B (2b,0), C (2c,2d)\n\nArea = 1/2 | (0*(0 - 2d) + 2b*(2d - 0) + 2c*(0 - 0) ) | = 1/2 |0 + 4b d + 0| = 1/2 * 4b d = 2b d\n\nSo the area of quadrilateral CDGE is (2b d)/3, and the area of triangle ABC is 2b d. Therefore, the ratio is (2b d /3) / (2b d) = 1/3.\n\nWait, that gives a ratio of 1/3. But that seems high. Wait, but let's check the calculation.\n\nWait, according to the shoelace formula, the sum was -4b d /3, so absolute value is 4b d /3, multiplied by 1/2 gives 2b d /3. Area of ABC is 2b d. So ratio is (2b d