ateral \\( CDGE \\):**\n   - Coordinates of \\( CDGE \\): \\( C(2c, 2d) \\), \\( D(b + c, d) \\), \\( G\\left( \\frac{2(b + c)}{3}, \\frac{2d}{3} \\right) \\), \\( E(c, d) \\).\n   - Using the shoelace formula, the area of \\( CDGE \\) is calculated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -2bd + 0 + \\frac{2bd}{3} + 0 \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -\\frac{4bd}{3} \\right| = \\frac{2bd}{3}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Area of Triangle \\( ABC \\):**\n   - Using the coordinates, the area of \\( ABC \\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| 0 + 4bd + 0 \\right| = 2bd\n     \\]\n\n4. **Ratio Calculation:**\n   - The ratio of the area of \\( CDGE \\) to the area of \\( ABC \\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\frac{2bd}{3}}{2bd} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\nVerification with specific coordinates (e.g., \\( A(0,0) \\), \\( B(6,0) \\), \\( C(0,6) \\)) confirms the ratio is consistent. Additionally, considering the centroid divides the triangle into six smaller triangles of equal area, quadrilateral \\( CDGE \\) comprises two such triangles, leading to the same ratio \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\).\n\nThus, the ratio of the area of quadrilateral \\( CDGE \\) to the area of triangle \\( ABC \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{3}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nIn triangle \\(ABC\\), the medians \\(AD\\) and \\(BE\\) meet at the centroid \\(G\\). Determine the ratio of the area of quadrilateral \\(CDGE\\) to the area of triangle \\(ABC\\).\n\n1. **Consider the medians and centroid:**\n   - Given a triangle \\(ABC\\), the medians \\(AD\\), \\(BE\\), and \\(CF\\) intersect at the centroid \\(G\\).\n   - The centroid divides each median into two segments, with the centroid \\(G\\) being \\(2/3\\) of the way along each median from a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n   - This property can be utilized in considering the areas of sub-triangles in \\(ABC\\).\n\n2. **Identify sub-triangles and relationships:**\n   - The area of triangle \\(ABC\\) can be d