 \\cos \\theta}{\\sin \\theta}x + 1\n     \\]\n   - After solving, the coordinates of \\( D \\) are found to be:\n     \\[\n     D \\left( \\frac{\\cos \\theta - 1 + \\sin \\theta}{2}, \\frac{\\sin \\theta (1 + \\cos \\theta + \\sin \\theta)}{2(1 + \\cos \\theta)} \\right)\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculating Distances**:\n   - Distance \\( BC \\) is \\( \\sqrt{2(1 + \\cos \\theta)} \\).\n   - Distance \\( AB \\) is \\( \\sqrt{2(1 - \\cos \\theta)} \\).\n\n6. **Simplifying \\( BD \\)**:\n   - Using trigonometric identities, express \\( BD \\) in terms of \\( \\theta \\):\n     \\[\n     BD = \\frac{1 + \\cos \\theta - \\sin \\theta}{\\sqrt{2(1 + \\cos \\theta)}}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify using half-angle identities:\n     \\[\n     BD = \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\right) - \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\right)\n     \\]\n\n7. **Final Comparison**:\n   - Show that \\( \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB) \\) simplifies to \\( \\cos \\left( \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\right) - \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\right) \\), which matches the simplified form of \\( BD \\).\n\nThus, we conclude that \\( BD = \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB) \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{BD = \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Given \\(M\\) is the midpoint of arc \\( \\overparen{AC} \\), \\(B\\) is an arbitrary point on arc \\( \\overparen{AM} \\), and \\(MD \\perp BC\\) at point \\(D\\). We need to prove that:\n\n\\[ BD = \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB). \\]\n\nTo approach this problem, we will use some geometric properties and congruence of right triangles. \n\n1. First, construct \\(ME \\perp AB\\) at point \\(E\\).\n2. Since \\(M\\) is the midpoint of arc \\( \\overparen{AC} \\) (which might imply that \\(M\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(C\\)), we can leverage symmetry.\n\n#### Step by Step Solution:\n\n1. **Recognizing Right Triangles and Their Properties**:\n   - Consider \\(\\triangle MAE\\) and \\(\\triangle MCD\\). Since \\(ME \\perp AB\\) and \\(MD \\perp BC\\), both triangles are right triangles.\n   - Observe that \\(\\angle MAE = \\angle MCD = 90^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Proving Triangle Congruence**:\n   - Given \\(M\\) is the midpoint of arc \\( \\overparen{AC} \\), \\(MA\\) is equal to \\(MC\\).\n   - We know \\(ME\\) is the perpendicular from \\(M\\) to line \\(AB\\), and similarly, \\(MD\\) is the perpendicular from \\(M\\) to line \\(BC\\).\n   - Since \\(MA = MC\\) and they share the length of \\(ME = MD\\) (perpendicular distances from \\(M\\) to the lines), we can use the Hypotenuse-Leg (HL) Theorem for right triangles.\n   - Thus, \\(\\triangle MAE \\cong \\triangle MCD\\).\n\n3. **Deduction from Congruent Triangles**:\n   - From \\(\\triangle MAE \\cong \\triangle MCD\\), it follows that \\(AE = CD\\).\n\n4. **Another Set of Congruent Triangles**:\n   - Consider \\(\\triangle MBE\\) and \\(\\triangle MBD\\).\n   - Using the same reasoning (right triangles, hypotenuse-leg), since \\(BE = BD\\), \\(\\triangle MBE \\cong \\triangle MBD\\).\n\n5. **Relating Segments**:\n   - From \\(\\triangle MBE\\) and \\(\\triangle MBD\\), \\(BD = BE\\).\n   - Recall: \\(CD = AE = AB + BE\\) (since \\(E\\) lies on \\(AB\\)).\n\n6. **Combining Relationships and Simplifying**:\n   - We know \\(BD = BE\\), and \\(CD = AE\\) can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   CD = AB + BE.\n   \\]\n   - Given \\(BE = BD\\), we rewrite:\n   \\[\n   CD = AB + BD.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Final Equation and Result**:\n   - Since \\(C\\) is also part of the line extending from \\(B\\) to \\(D\\) and \\(M\\) is the midpoint of arc \\( \\overparen{AC}\\), \\(D\\) must lie midway between points such that:\n   \\[\n   BC = CD + BD.\n   \\]\n   - Using the previous equation for \\(CD\\):\n   \\[\n   BC = (AB + BD) + BD,\n   \\]\n   - Simplifying for \\(BD\\):\n   \\[\n   BC = AB + 2BD \\implies 2BD = BC - AB \\implies BD = \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB).\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{BD = \\frac{1}{2}(BC - AB)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"pro