y football: \\( 20 - x \\).\n   - Number of students playing only volleyball: \\( 19 - x \\).\n   - Number of students playing exactly one of the sports is given as 15.\n\n3. **Set up the Equation for Exactly One Sport**:\n   \\[\n   (20 - x) + (19 - x) = 15\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\)**:\n   \\[\n   (20 - x) + (19 - x) = 15 \\\\\n   39 - 2x = 15 \\\\\n   39 - 15 = 2x \\\\\n   24 = 2x \\\\\n   x = \\frac{24}{2} \\\\\n   x = 12\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the Total Number Practicing At Least One Sport**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number practicing at least one sport} = 20 + 19 - x = 20 + 19 - 12 = 27\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the Number of Students Who Do Not Play Any Sport**:\n   - Total number of students: 40.\n   - Students playing at least one sport: 27.\n\n   Therefore, the number of students who do not play any sport:\n   \\[\n   40 - 27 = 13\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the true absolute error of the number \\( a_{0} = 245.2 \\) if \\( a = 246 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the true absolute error of the number a\u2080 = 245.2 when the true value a is 246. Let me start by recalling what absolute error means. From what I remember, absolute error is the difference between the measured or approximated value and the actual or true value. The formula should be something like absolute error = |true value - approximate value|. \n\nWait, let me make sure. If a is the true value and a\u2080 is the approximate value, then the absolute error is |a - a\u2080|. Right, so plugging in the numbers here, that would be |246 - 245.2|. Let me compute that. 246 minus 245.2 is 0.8. Taking the absolute value, which is still 0.8. So the absolute error is 0.8. Is that all? Hmm, maybe I should double-check.\n\nWait, absolute error is just the magnitude of the difference, so regardless of which one is bigger, we take the positive valu