 2x y^2\\). Factor out x y:\n\n\\(x y (3x - 2y)\\)\n\nSo, the function is \\(f(x,y) = x y (3x - 2y)\\)\n\nHmm, interesting. So, since x and y are in [0,1], the sign of the function depends on the term (3x - 2y). Let's analyze when 3x - 2y is positive or negative.\n\n3x - 2y > 0 => y < (3/2)x\n\nSimilarly, 3x - 2y < 0 => y > (3/2)x\n\nGiven that x and y are in [0,1], let's see the region where y < (3/2)x. Since y \u22641 and x \u22641, the line y = (3/2)x intersects the square at x = 2/3, y=1. So, above that line (for x > 2/3), y can be up to 1, but the line y=(3/2)x would go beyond y=1 when x > 2/3.\n\nWait, actually, for x in [0, 2/3], y = (3/2)x \u22641. For x > 2/3, y=(3/2)x would be greater than 1, which is outside the square. So, within the square [0,1]x[0,1], the line y=(3/2)x is from (0,0) to (2/3,1). Above this line (i.e., for y > (3/2)x), the term (3x - 2y) becomes negative.\n\nTherefore, the function f(x,y) is positive when y < (3/2)x and negative when y > (3/2)x. Along the line y=(3/2)x, the function is zero.\n\nSo, the maximum is likely to occur in the region where the function is positive, and the minimum where it's negative.\n\nTo find the maximum, we need to maximize x y (3x - 2y) where y < (3/2)x.\n\nSimilarly, the minimum would be the minimum of x y (3x - 2y) where y > (3/2)x.\n\nBut since we already did the analysis on the edges and found the critical points, maybe this factorization doesn't add much, but it's useful for understanding the behavior.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use Lagrange multipliers on the boundaries. Wait, but since we already converted the boundary edges into single-variable functions and found their extrema, maybe that's sufficient.\n\nBut let me check another thing. Suppose we consider the interior of the domain. Since the only critical point is at (0,0), which is a saddle point or a local min/max? Let's check the second derivative test.\n\nCompute the second partial derivatives:\n\n\\(f_{xx} = 6y\\)\n\n\\(f_{yy} = -4x\\)\n\n\\(